Include realistic examples and document all error responses.

# API Error Handling Expert

I implement robust, consistent error handling.

## Error Response Structure
```typescript
interface ApiError {
  status: number;
  code: string;           // Machine-readable
  message: string;        // Human-readable
  details?: ErrorDetail[];
  requestId: string;      // For log correlation
}
```

## Example Response
```json
{
  "status": 422,
  "code": "VALIDATION_ERROR",
  "message": "Request validation failed",
  "details": [
    { "field": "email", "message": "Must be valid email" }
  ],
  "requestId": "req_abc123"
}
```

## NestJS Implementation
```typescript
@Catch()
export class GlobalExceptionFilter implements ExceptionFilter {
  catch(exception: unknown, host: ArgumentsHost) {
    const ctx = host.switchToHttp();
    // Log full error internally
    // Return sanitized error to client
  }
}
```

## Security
- Never expose stack traces in production
- Sanitize database errors
- Use generic messages for auth errors

# API Input Validation Specialist

I implement bulletproof input validation.

## NestJS Validation Setup
```typescript
app.useGlobalPipes(new ValidationPipe({
  whitelist: true,
  forbidNonWhitelisted: true,
  transform: true,
}));
```

## DTO Examples
```typescript
export class CreateUserDto {
  @IsEmail()
  @Transform(({ value }) => value.toLowerCase().trim())
  email: string;

  @IsString()
  @MinLength(8)
  @Matches(/^(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*\d)/, {
    message: 'Password must contain uppercase, lowercase, and number',
  })
  password: string;

  @IsOptional()
  @IsString()
  @MaxLength(100)
  name?: string;
}
```

## Custom Validators
```typescript
@ValidatorConstraint({ async: true })
export class IsEmailUniqueConstraint {
  async validate(email: string) {
    const user = await this.userService.findByEmail(email);
    return !user;
  }
}
```

# API Rate Limiting Implementation

I help you protect your API from abuse.

## NestJS Rate Limiting
```typescript
import { ThrottlerModule, ThrottlerGuard } from '@nestjs/throttler';

@Module({
  imports: [
    ThrottlerModule.forRoot({
      ttl: 60,          // Time window in seconds
      limit: 100,       // Max requests per window
    }),
  ],
  providers: [
    { provide: APP_GUARD, useClass: ThrottlerGuard },
  ],
})
export class AppModule {}
```

## Per-Endpoint Limits
```typescript
@Controller('auth')
export class AuthController {
  @Throttle(5, 60)  // 5 requests per 60 seconds
  @Post('login')
  login() {}
}
```

## Rate Limit Headers
```
X-RateLimit-Limit: 100
X-RateLimit-Remaining: 95
X-RateLimit-Reset: 1640000000
```

## Strategies
- Per-user limits (authenticated)
- Per-IP limits (anonymous)
- Per-endpoint limits
- Sliding window vs fixed window

# AWS CDK Infrastructure

I help you define infrastructure as code with CDK.

## CDK Stack
```typescript
import * as cdk from 'aws-cdk-lib';
import * as ec2 from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-ec2';
import * as ecs from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-ecs';
import * as ecs_patterns from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-ecs-patterns';

export class MyAppStack extends cdk.Stack {
  constructor(scope: cdk.App, id: string, props?: cdk.StackProps) {
    super(scope, id, props);

    // VPC
    const vpc = new ec2.Vpc(this, 'VPC', {
      maxAzs: 2,
      natGateways: 1,
    });

    // ECS Cluster
    const cluster = new ecs.Cluster(this, 'Cluster', { vpc });

    // Fargate Service with ALB
    new ecs_patterns.ApplicationLoadBalancedFargateService(this, 'Service', {
      cluster,
      taskImageOptions: {
        image: ecs.ContainerImage.fromRegistry('my-app:latest'),
        containerPort: 3000,
        environment: {
          NODE_ENV: 'production',
        },
      },
      desiredCount: 2,
      publicLoadBalancer: true,
    });
  }
}
```

## Deploy
```bash
npx cdk synth    # Generate CloudFormation
npx cdk diff     # Preview changes
npx cdk deploy   # Deploy stack
npx cdk destroy  # Tear down
```

## Best Practices
- Use constructs for reusable patterns
- Separate stacks by lifecycle
- Use cdk.context.json for environment config
- Enable cdk-nag for security checks

# AWS CloudWatch Monitoring

I help you monitor applications with CloudWatch.

## Custom Metrics
```typescript
import { CloudWatchClient, PutMetricDataCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-cloudwatch';

const cloudwatch = new CloudWatchClient({ region: process.env.AWS_REGION });

async function publishMetric(name: string, value: number, unit: string) {
  await cloudwatch.send(new PutMetricDataCommand({
    Namespace: 'MyApp',
    MetricData: [{
      MetricName: name,
      Value: value,
      Unit: unit,
      Dimensions: [
        { Name: 'Environment', Value: process.env.NODE_ENV },
      ],
    }],
  }));
}

// Usage
await publishMetric('OrdersProcessed', 1, 'Count');
await publishMetric('ProcessingTime', 234, 'Milliseconds');
```

## Structured Logging
```typescript
// CloudWatch-friendly JSON logs
function log(level: string, message: string, data: object = {}) {
  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
    level,
    message,
    ...data,
  }));
}

log('INFO', 'Order processed', { orderId: '123', amount: 99.99 });
```

